<section id="purpose">
<h2><span class="n">01</span>Purpose and requirements</h2>
<p>As Aerodynamics Subteam Lead on Cornell's AIAA Design Build Fly team, I work mostly in simulation. Our team has no physical way to check a CFD result against real flow. I wanted a desktop tunnel that could put small DBF wing segments in a uniform airstream, show flow behavior with tufts and fog, and teach me how a real test facility is sized.</p>
<p>Before any calculation, I set the constraints the design had to meet:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Target</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Footprint</td><td>Fits on a coffee table, under about 40 in</td><td>Must live in an apartment, not a lab</td></tr>
<tr><td>Manufacturing</td><td>Printable on a Bambu A1 (256 mm bed)</td><td>Sets the maximum part size and drives the modular layout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Test article</td><td>Wing segments around 4 in chord</td><td>Sets test-section size and Reynolds number</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flow quality</td><td>Uniform, low-turbulence core</td><td>Without it, measurements are noise</td></tr>
<tr><td>Visualization</td><td>Clear windows and a smoke source</td><td>Makes flow features visible and photographable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drive</td><td>Low-voltage 120 mm fans</td><td>Safe, cheap, quiet, and fits the packaging</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
</section>
<section id="feasibility">
<h2><span class="n">02</span>First-pass sizing: why 30 m/s was the wrong goal</h2>
<p>My first concept was an 8 × 8 in test section running at 30 m/s, driven by two computer fans. A quick power estimate shows why that concept could not work. The required volume flow is the test-section area times the speed:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ Q = A\,V = (0.0413\ \text{m}^2)(30\ \text{m/s}) = 1.24\ \text{m}^3/\text{s} \approx 2600\ \text{CFM} \]</div>
<p>The test-section dynamic pressure, and a typical small-tunnel loss of 1.3 to 2 times that value, set the pressure the fan must supply:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ q = \tfrac12 \rho V^2 = \tfrac12(1.225)(30)^2 = 551\ \text{Pa}, \qquad \Delta p_{\text{fan}} \approx 700\text{–}1100\ \text{Pa} \]</div>
<div class="eq">\[ P = \frac{Q\,\Delta p}{\eta} \approx \frac{(1.24)(900)}{0.5} \approx 2.2\ \text{kW} \]</div>
<p>Two case fans supply roughly 10 W. The concept was short by a factor of about 200 in power. The choice was to buy a 3 hp blower or to change the goal.</p>
<div class="decision"><span class="k">Decision</span><p>Keep the low-voltage fan drive and the coffee-table footprint. Drop the speed target and design the geometry for flow quality, then let the fan curve set the operating speed.</p></div>
<h3>Design iterations</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Inlet → test section</th><th class="num">CR</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A</td><td>No contraction, 8 × 8 in, honeycomb at test-section entry</td><td class="num">1.0</td><td>A duct, not a tunnel. Rejected on power and flow quality.</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>10 × 10 → 6 × 6 in</td><td class="num">2.78</td><td>Too little turbulence reduction for the length</td></tr>
<tr><td>Final</td><td>9.5 × 9.5 → 5 × 5 in</td><td class="num">3.61</td><td>Best contraction that fits the fan array and print bed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
</section>
<section id="architecture">
<h2><span class="n">03</span>Architecture: open-circuit suction</h2>
<p>I chose an open-circuit layout because a closed return circuit would double the footprint. I placed the fans at the downstream end so they pull air through the tunnel instead of pushing it. A blower-type tunnel sends fan swirl and blade wakes straight into the test section. In a suction tunnel the air the model sees has only passed through the honeycomb, screen and contraction.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Section</th><th class="num">Length (in)</th><th>Cross-section</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Settling module</td><td class="num">4.0</td><td>9.5 in square</td><td>Honeycomb, screen, settling length</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contraction</td><td class="num">8.5</td><td>9.5 → 5 in</td><td>Accelerates flow, damps turbulence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Test section</td><td class="num">9.0</td><td>5 in square, 0.5° draft</td><td>Model and viewing window</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diffuser</td><td class="num">13.0</td><td>5 → 6.5 in</td><td>Recovers pressure, isolates fans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Step + plenum</td><td class="num">2.2</td><td>10.5 in square</td><td>Spreads flow across all four fans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fan plate</td><td class="num">0.5</td><td>2 × 2, bellmouthed bores</td><td>Drive</td></tr>
<tr class="tot"><td>Total</td><td class="num">37.2</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
</section>
<section id="conditioning">
<h2><span class="n">04</span>Flow conditioning: honeycomb and screen</h2>
<p>Room air enters with swirl and large eddies. The honeycomb straightens it, and the screen evens out the velocity profile. Both sit in the settling chamber, where the air is slowest. Their pressure loss scales with local dynamic pressure, so at one-thirteenth of the test-section <span class="m">q</span> they cost very little.</p>
<h3>Honeycomb</h3>
<p>I followed Mehta and Bradshaw's rule of a cell length-to-diameter ratio of 6 to 8. I used 1/8 in (3.2 mm) hexagonal cells, 1.0 in deep, for <span class="m">L/d = 8</span>. At the design speed the air moves through the cells at about 2.4 m/s, so the cell Reynolds number is low and the flow inside each cell stays laminar:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ Re_{\text{cell}} = \frac{V_s\,d}{\nu} = \frac{(2.41)(0.00318)}{1.5\times10^{-5}} \approx 510 \]</div>
<h3>Screen</h3>
<p>A screen corrects non-uniform flow because its pressure drop is highest where the local velocity is highest. This only works when the open-area ratio <span class="m">β</span> is at least 0.57. Below that value the flow through the screen becomes unstable and the screen adds non-uniformity instead of removing it. I selected standard fiberglass window screen (18 × 16 weave, 0.011 in thread) and checked it:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ \beta = \left(1 - \frac{d}{p_1}\right)\left(1 - \frac{d}{p_2}\right) = (1 - 0.198)(1 - 0.176) = 0.66 \;>\; 0.57 \]</div>
<p>The screen sits 2 in behind the honeycomb. That is 0.21 chamber widths, which matches the recommended honeycomb-to-screen spacing. The screen is clamped in a two-ring frame that stretches it like an embroidery hoop, because a slack screen opens up where velocity is high and reverses its own corrective effect.</p>
<div class="decision"><span class="k">Trade-off</span><p>The screen-to-contraction gap is 1 in, about half the textbook 0.2 chamber widths. I accepted it to keep the settling module at 4 in. The turbulence the screen creates keeps decaying through the 8.5 in contraction, so the effective decay length before the test section is closer to 9.5 in.</p></div>
