<section id="contraction">
<h2><span class="n">05</span>Contraction</h2>
<p>The contraction accelerates the flow and is the main reason the test-section flow is uniform. Rapid-distortion theory shows how a contraction of ratio <span class="m">c</span> scales turbulence intensity:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ \frac{u'/U\big|_{\text{exit}}}{u'/U\big|_{\text{inlet}}} \sim \frac{1}{c^{2}} \ \text{(axial)}, \qquad \sim \frac{1}{\sqrt{c}} \ \text{(lateral)} \]</div>
<p>The ratio itself is set by the inlet and exit widths:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ c = \left(\frac{w_i}{w_e}\right)^{2} = \left(\frac{9.5}{5}\right)^{2} = 3.61 \]</div>
<p>At <span class="m">c = 3.61</span>, axial turbulence intensity drops to about 8% of its inlet value and lateral intensity to about 53%. The usual recommendation for small tunnels is 6 to 9. The inlet width was capped by the 2 × 2 fan array (9.45 in across) and the print bed, so I could not reach that range. The screen carries more of the lateral smoothing as a result.</p>
<h3>Length and wall shape</h3>
<p>A short contraction risks separation at the wide end. A long one thickens the boundary layer and adds friction. Bell and Mehta's guidance is a length of about 0.7 to 1.0 times the inlet width. I used 8.5 in, which gives <span class="m">L/w<sub>i</sub> = 0.89</span>.</p>
<h3>Deriving the wall equation</h3>
<p>With the end widths and length fixed, the wall shape has to meet six conditions. The half-width must equal <span class="m">h<sub>i</sub> = 4.75 in</span> at the inlet and <span class="m">h<sub>e</sub> = 2.5 in</span> at the exit. The slope must be zero at both ends so the wall runs parallel to the settling chamber and test section. The curvature must also be zero at both ends, because a sudden change in curvature causes a local pressure spike that can separate the boundary layer at the wide end or trip it near the exit.</p>
<p>Six conditions need six coefficients, which is a fifth-order polynomial. Writing it in the non-dimensional coordinate <span class="m">ξ = x/L</span> and applying the conditions gives:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ h(\xi) = h_i - (h_i - h_e)\left(6\xi^5 - 15\xi^4 + 10\xi^3\right), \qquad \xi = \frac{x}{L} \]</div>
<p>The polynomial is symmetric about its midpoint. The inflection point sits at <span class="m">x = L/2</span>, where the half-width is 3.625 in. The steepest wall slope occurs there:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ \left.\frac{dh}{dx}\right|_{\max} = \frac{15}{8}\,\frac{h_i - h_e}{L} = \frac{15}{8}\,\frac{2.25}{8.5} = 0.50 \;\Rightarrow\; 26^\circ \]</div>
<p>I used that value to check the length choice. A shorter contraction steepens the wall and strengthens the adverse pressure gradient on the concave section near the inlet, where separation is most likely. At 8.5 in the peak wall angle stays moderate while keeping the overall tunnel under 40 in. I entered the polynomial as an equation-driven curve in SolidWorks, driven by the global variables for <span class="m">h<sub>i</sub></span>, <span class="m">h<sub>e</sub></span> and <span class="m">L</span>, and applied the same profile to both planes of the square duct.</p>
