<section id="test">
<h2><span class="n">06</span>Test section</h2>
<p>The test section is 5 × 5 in and 9 in long, 1.8 hydraulic diameters. That leaves the model clear of the contraction exit with room behind it for the wake. The walls diverge by 0.5°. The boundary layer grows along the section and shrinks the effective core, which would otherwise accelerate the flow. The draft holds the core velocity constant, so a reference reading upstream matches the speed at the model.</p>
<p>For a 4 in chord at the design speed, the chord Reynolds number is in the range DBF aircraft actually fly at:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ Re_c = \frac{V c}{\nu} = \frac{(8.7)(0.1016)}{1.5\times10^{-5}} \approx 59{,}000 \]</div>
<h3>Viewing window</h3>
<p>My first approach was to pause the print partway and embed a 4 × 6 in acrylic panel in the wall. The panel did not seat or seal well, and it would have been permanent. I redesigned the window as a separate 3D-printed retainer that press-fits the acrylic into the wall opening. No fasteners pass through the acrylic, so it is never stressed at a bolt hole, and the panel can be removed for cleaning or replacement. The retainer holds the acrylic so its inner face sits in line with the tunnel wall, because a recessed or protruding window would shed vortices beside the model.</p>
