<section id="diffuser">
<h2><span class="n">07</span>Diffuser, step and plenum</h2>
<p>The diffuser slows the air after the test section and converts kinetic energy back into static pressure, which reduces the pressure the fans must supply. Its limit is the wall angle. Past an equivalent cone half-angle of about 4 to 5° the flow separates intermittently. That unsteadiness travels upstream into the test section. For a square duct, I converted to the equal-area circle with <span class="m">D = 1.128w</span>:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ \theta_{\text{wall}} = \tan^{-1}\!\frac{6.5 - 5}{2(13)} = 3.30^\circ, \qquad \theta_{\text{eq}} = \tan^{-1}\!\frac{1.128(6.5-5)}{2(13)} = 3.72^\circ \]</div>
<div class="eq">\[ AR = \frac{6.5^2}{5^2} = 1.69, \qquad C_{p,\text{ideal}} = 1 - \frac{1}{AR^2} = 0.65 \]</div>
<div class="decision"><span class="k">Decision</span><p>A 9 in diffuser would have delivered about 97% of the 13 in version's speed. I kept 13 in to put distance between the model and the fans. Four axial fans create swirl and a four-lobed suction pattern, and that pressure field reaches upstream. With 13 in of diffuser plus the plenum, the model sits about 20 in, or four test-section widths, ahead of the fan face.</p></div>
<h3>Step to the plenum</h3>
<p>Going from 6.5 in to the 10.5 in plenum with a proper diffuser would take another 40 in, so I used a flat bulkhead, a deliberate sudden expansion. Its loss follows Borda–Carnot:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ K = \left(1 - \frac{A_1}{A_2}\right)^{2} = \left(1 - \frac{42.25}{110.25}\right)^{2} = 0.38 \]</div>
<p>I kept the step edge sharp. A sharp edge fixes the separation line in place, so the recirculation is steady and repeatable. A rounded edge lets the separation point move with flow rate. A 2 in plenum after the step lets the jet spread across all four fans so they share the load evenly. The plenum is 10.5 in square so no fan bore is partly blocked by a wall.</p>
</section>
<section id="fans">
<h2><span class="n">08</span>Loss budget and fan matching</h2>
<p>The operating speed of any tunnel is where the fan curve crosses the system resistance curve. I built the resistance curve from a component loss budget. Each loss is <span class="m">Δp<sub>i</sub> = K<sub>i</sub>q<sub>i</sub></span> at that component's local speed. Referencing every term to the test section through continuity gives:</p>
<div class="eq">\[ \Delta p_{\text{sys}} = q_t \sum_i K_i \left(\frac{A_t}{A_i}\right)^{2} \]</div>
