<section id="build">
<h2><span class="n">09</span>CAD and fabrication</h2>
<p>I modeled the tunnel in SolidWorks as seven part files in one assembly, all driven by a shared set of global variables (<span class="m">W_set</span>, <span class="m">W_test</span>, <span class="m">L_cont</span>, and others) linked from one external file. Changing the test-section width updates every part. The contraction uses an equation-driven curve and a lofted boss with a thin feature for the wall. The honeycomb is a hexagonal sketch pattern on a 60° offset.</p>
<ul class="clean">
<li><b>Print-bed limit.</b> The diffuser was the only module larger than the 256 mm bed. I split it into two parts and glued them together. Every other module printed in one piece.</li>
<li><b>Fasteners.</b> I first planned 1/4 in bolts, but their flange tabs pushed parts past the bed size, so I switched to M3. Clearance holes are modeled at 3.7 mm so they print near the 3.4 mm normal fit. Captive-nut pockets are modeled at 5.8 mm across flats.</li>
<li><b>Material.</b> PETG, printed on a Bambu A1.</li>
<li><b>Cost.</b> Off-the-shelf fans, window screen and acrylic. Other parts were designed around material already on hand.</li>
</ul>
