<section id="results">
<h2><span class="n">11</span>Commissioning and results</h2>
<p>I measured a maximum of <b>2.9 m/s</b> at the center of the test section, about 33% of the 8.7 m/s design speed. At this speed a 4 in chord runs at <span class="m">Re ≈ 20,000</span>.</p>
<h3>Diagnosis</h3>
<p>The tunnel was built to the design dimensions. The difference is in the fans. The installed fans draw 0.3 A at 12 V, 3.6 W each. They are standard case fans, rated by free-air flow. The design assumed high-static-pressure fans that draw 0.6 to 2.5 A and develop 40 to 100 Pa. A case fan's flow drops steeply once it pushes against any resistance.</p>
<p>The system curve explains the result. At 2.9 m/s the tunnel needs only</p>
<div class="eq">\[ \Delta p_{\text{sys}} = 0.29\,(2.9)^2 \approx 2.5\ \text{Pa} \]</div>
<p>so the installed fans can deliver only about 2.5 Pa at the flow they are moving. The loss budget does not need to be wrong for the result to make sense. The fans are pressure-limited.</p>
<h3>Model versus prototype</h3>
<p>The table compares what the design model predicted with what the physical tunnel delivered. The predicted values assume the high-static fan from section 08. The "at measured speed" column applies the same model to the measured 2.9 m/s, which shows what the tunnel is actually doing internally.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th class="num">Design model</th><th class="num">At measured speed</th><th class="num">Change</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Test-section speed</td><td class="num">8.7 m/s</td><td class="num">2.9 m/s</td><td class="num">−67%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Volume flow</td><td class="num">297 CFM</td><td class="num">99 CFM</td><td class="num">−67%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Test-section dynamic pressure</td><td class="num">46 Pa</td><td class="num">5.2 Pa</td><td class="num">−89%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fan pressure rise needed</td><td class="num">22 Pa</td><td class="num">2.5 Pa</td><td class="num">−89%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Settling-chamber speed</td><td class="num">2.4 m/s</td><td class="num">0.80 m/s</td><td class="num">−67%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chord Reynolds number (4 in)</td><td class="num">59,000</td><td class="num">20,000</td><td class="num">−67%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Because pressure scales with the square of speed, a 67% shortfall in speed is an 89% shortfall in the pressure the fans are producing. The geometry was built to the design dimensions, so most of the gap traces back to the fans, not the tunnel. The Reynolds number result matters most for testing. At 22,000 a wing section sits in the regime where laminar separation bubbles dominate, so lift and stall behavior will differ from the DBF flight condition and cannot be scaled up directly.</p>
<h3>Sources of error</h3>
<p>The model and the measurement each carry uncertainty. I sorted the main sources by which side of the comparison they affect and which way they push the result.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Effect</th><th>Direction</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><b>Fan curve assumption</b></td><td>The design used a linearized high-static fan curve. The installed fans are 0.3 A case fans whose curve under load is unknown. This is the largest single source of error.</td><td>Model over-predicts speed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Fan installation effects</b></td><td>Four fans in parallel draw from one short plenum with non-uniform inflow. Fans in this arrangement deliver less pressure than their free-standing rating.</td><td>Model over-predicts speed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Low-Reynolds loss coefficients</b></td><td>The K values come from higher-Reynolds tunnels. The screen wire Reynolds number here is about 15, and the test-section Reynolds number based on width is about 25,000. Screen loss and wall friction both rise at low Reynolds number.</td><td>Model under-predicts losses</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Diffuser performance</b></td><td>A thick boundary layer entering the diffuser at low speed separates more easily than the 85% efficiency assumes, especially with a 3.7° equivalent angle.</td><td>Model under-predicts losses</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Air leakage</b></td><td>A suction tunnel runs below room pressure, so any gap at a module joint, the glued diffuser seam, or the press-fit window draws air in. Air entering downstream of the test section raises fan flow without raising test-section speed.</td><td>Lowers measured speed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Surface finish and tolerance</b></td><td>FDM layer lines add wall roughness and skin friction. Dimensional error of a few tenths of a millimeter changes the test-section area and contraction ratio slightly.</td><td>Small; lowers measured speed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Single-point measurement</b></td><td>Speed was read at one point at the test-section center. The core runs faster than the cross-section average, so one reading does not give the true volume flow.</td><td>Measured value is not an average</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Anemometer accuracy</b></td><td>Handheld anemometers typically carry a few percent plus a fixed offset, and 2.9 m/s is near the low end of their range. The probe head also blocks part of a 5 in section and accelerates the flow around it.</td><td>Roughly ±5–10% on the reading</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Air properties</b></td><td>Calculations use sea-level standard density and viscosity. Room temperature and humidity shift both by a few percent.</td><td>Small; affects Re and q</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Taken together, the fan-related errors explain most of the gap. The loss-related errors push in the same direction, which means even with correct fans the tunnel would likely land somewhat below 8.7 m/s. A pitot traverse across the section and a datasheet fan curve would narrow both the measurement and the model uncertainty.</p>
