<section id="next">
<h2><span class="n">12</span>Lessons and next steps</h2>
<h3>What I learned</h3>
<ul class="clean">
<li><b>Specify fans by static pressure, not CFM.</b> CFM is measured at zero back-pressure and says almost nothing about performance in a tunnel. The operating point is set by where the fan curve crosses the system curve.</li>
<li><b>Packaging drives the aerodynamics.</b> The fan array width set the plenum, the plenum set the contraction inlet, and the inlet set the contraction ratio. Each compromise has a cost I could calculate before printing.</li>
<li><b>Put the expensive parts where the air is slow.</b> The honeycomb's contribution to the loss budget is under 0.04 because it sits at one-thirteenth of the test-section dynamic pressure.</li>
<li><b>Design for revision.</b> The modular, parametric CAD let me redesign the window and settling module without reprinting the rest of the tunnel.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Next steps</h3>
<ul class="clean">
<li>Replace the drive with high-static 120 mm fans rated at 40 Pa or more. The fan-matching model predicts 7 to 9 m/s with no other changes.</li>
<li>Map velocity across the test section with a pitot traverse on a 5 × 5 grid. That gives a true average flow and a uniformity figure for the core.</li>
<li>Seal every joint and seam, then compare the settling-chamber speed with the continuity prediction of test speed ÷ 3.61 to confirm the tunnel is airtight.</li>
<li>Run tuft and fog visualization on a DBF wing section and compare the stall behavior with our XFLR5 and ANSYS Fluent predictions.</li>
</ul>
</section>
